# Cloudflare Pages redirects.
#
# Structure:
#   /                   encrypted landing (js-auth)
#   /product.html       product page (encrypted)
#   /docs/              docs tree (encrypted, multi-page)
#   /brand.html         brand book (encrypted)
#   /playground.html    email-gate page (encrypted)
#   /playground/        the dashboards app (NOT encrypted — gated by email)

# Friendly short paths.
#
# Targets are the URL Cloudflare actually serves (extensionless for files,
# trailing slash for directories). Pointing an alias at /playground.html made
# it a 302 into Pages' own 308 -> /playground, i.e. two hops for one click.
#
# /demo is NOT an alias any more: landing/demo/index.html is a real 75 KB page
# and this rule was shadowing it, so /demo was unreachable at its own URL.
# The dashboards gate keeps /gate and /dashboards.
/gate         /playground         301
/dashboards   /playground         301
/app          /playground/        301
/bench        /benchmarks/        301
/github       https://github.com/xerj-org/xerj 302

# Trailing slash normalization
/home         /                   301

# ── NO CATCH-ALL. Ever. ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# There must never be a rule whose source matches every path — `/* ... 200`,
# `/* ... 301`, `/:slug ...`. Cloudflare Pages decides not-found handling from
# the published tree: with a top-level `404.html` an unmatched path gets that
# page and a real HTTP 404; a catch-all here takes the path first and the 404
# never happens. That is a soft 404 — every typo'd URL becomes a duplicate of
# whatever the rule points at, and a removed page can no longer be signalled
# as removed. Until 2026-08-22 this site had no `404.html` at all and Pages
# treated it as a single-page app: `/`, `/answers/what-is-xerj` and
# `/this-page-has-never-existed-xyz123` all returned 200 with the same
# 21,841-byte homepage body and the same ETag.
#
# `seo_lint.py` rule 67 fails the build on a catch-all source and on a missing
# `landing/404.html`. Named prefixes (`/docs/*`) are fine: they claim a real
# section instead of claiming everything.
